But despite the reward and investigative efforts, leads were already drying up. Almost two weeks after Mark vanished, his disappearance was featured in a 10-minute segment of Fox Television's America's Most Wanted that was broadcast in the U.S. and Mexico, generating a total of 2,000 calls from the public. A Mexican woman called to implicate her boyfriend, a Matamoros police officer who spoke of arresting a drunk American, fitting Mark's description. After a failed attempt to extort money from the American, the officer shot him. As police corruption was a serious problem in Mexico, investigators honed in on an officer with ties to the criminal underworld, but he was ultimately ruled out. Calls placed Mark at various Mexican beaches, while some saw him in the company of a woman, and others claimed he was restrained. Investigators noticed that references to occult rituals and sacrifices kept cropping up, including a letter delivered to police that was covered in scribblings of pentagrams and other symbols. It featured a single sentence in Spanish that translated to, Mark was not the first.
但尽管有悬赏和调查努力，线索已经开始枯竭。在马克失踪近两周后，他的失踪案被搬上了福克斯电视台《全美通缉令》节目的一段 10 分钟片段，在美国和墨西哥播出，共引发了公众的 2,000 通电话。一名墨西哥妇女打电话来牵连她的男友，后者是马塔莫罗斯的一名警察，曾谈到逮捕了一名符合马克特征的醉酒美国人。在向美国人勒索金钱未遂后，这名警察开枪打死了他。由于警察腐败是墨西哥的一个严重问题，调查人员将目标锁定在与犯罪黑社会有联系的一名警察身上，但他最终被排除。打来的电话将马克安置在各个墨西哥海滩，一些人看到他和一个女人在一起，还有人声称他被拘禁了。调查人员注意到，关于神秘仪式和献祭的说法不断出现，包括一封交到警察局的信，上面涂满了五角星和其他符号。信中有一句西班牙语翻译过来就是：“马克不是第一个。”

