Investigators traced the calls to phones inside the Galveston County Jail in Texas. They were positioned in the souls of two inmates, Robert Miller and Wilton Smith, and were only supposed to be used to contact family and attorneys. Both men were serving time for burglary, and Miller had previously been charged with extortion after attempting to scam money from the family of missing teenager Shelly Sykes, who had disappeared three years earlier. Assured by government officials that efforts to find his son would persist, James Kilroy returned home. Mayor Jack Long encouraged Santa Fe residents to wear yellow ribbons in support of the Kilroy family, with benefits, auctions, garage sales and bake sales held to increase the reward pool. US customs agent Oren Neck told the media, This is definitely one of the most difficult cases I've ever dealt with. There appears to be no motive, no nothing. On Saturday, April 1, police in Matamoros set up a routine traffic stop on Mexican Federal Highway 2 to check passing cars but narcotics.
调查人员将这些电话追溯到德克萨斯州加尔维斯顿县监狱内的电话。它们原本只被放置在两名囚犯罗伯特·米勒和威尔顿·Smith的牢房里，且只应该用于联系家人和律师。这两名男子都在因入室盗窃服刑，米勒此前曾因试图向三年前失踪的失踪青少年雪莉·赛克斯的家人诈骗钱财而被指控敲诈勒索。在政府官员向其保证搜寻其儿子的努力将继续下去后，詹姆斯·基尔罗伊回到了家中。市长杰克·朗鼓励圣达菲居民佩戴黄丝带以支持基尔罗伊一家，并举办了慈善义演、拍卖、车库售卖和烘焙义卖来增加悬赏金额。美国海关特工奥伦·内克对媒体表示：“这绝对是我处理过的最困难的案件之一。似乎没有任何动机，什么都没有。”4 月 1 日星期六，马塔莫罗斯警方在墨西哥 2 号联邦公路设立了例行交通检查站，以检查过往车辆是否有毒品。

