Meanwhile, the statue in the blue Chevrolet was identified as a legua, a guard from Palo, an Afro-Cuban religion devoted to earthly powers and the worship of spirits. A legua was considered a trickster and a deity of the roads. Worshipers believed making offerings to him would protect them on their travels. Just over a week later on Sunday, April 9, police stormed Rancho Santa Elena and found 64 pounds of marijuana and several firearms. Seraphine, his uncle Elio, and two other men, David, Serna, Valdez, and Sergio Martinez Salinas, were placed under arrest for drug trafficking. The four men were interrogated into the early hours, but each maintained relaxed demeanors and refused to answer questions. The ranch's caretaker, Domingo Bustamante, arrived for work that morning unaware that his employers had been arrested. When questioned by police, Domingo said roughly three to four weeks prior, a tall blonde-haired young man was in the back of the blue Chevrolet suburban, bound and blindfolded. The captive spoke English, which Domingo couldn't understand as he only spoke Spanish.
